('ioi.zone', 10, 'International Object Identifier (IOI)', 1, 1, '{\"contact\":null,\"about\":null,\"privacy\":null,\"terms_conditions\":null}', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '<h4>International Object Identifier (IOI)</h4>                                                                   <h6>International Object Identifier (IOI) is a permanent address to an scholarly online article. The International Object Identifier (IOI) is a modern article address. IOI is a fixed permanent international article address which auto-finds the article new address                        </h6>                                                                            <h4>IOI solves the following common problems of online articles:                         </h4>                                              <div style=\"margin-top:15px; font-size:14px; line-height:20px\">                                                <ul><li>IOI keeps article permanently online via permanent IAA address</li>                             <li>IOI auto-detects any change in article online address and auto-adjusts itself to it.</li>                             <li>IOI auto-detects broken article URL or removed article, and load metadata instead temporary.</li>                             <li>IOI extends article lifetime by keeping article metadata and abstracted data permanently online.</li>                             <li>IOI allow improvement of article allowing reader to modify article can be accept/rejected by the publisher.</li>                           </ul>                                              </div>                                                   <h4>Accessing Article by IOI:</h4>                                              <p>An IOI is a permanent address to an specific article  (example: IOI.zone/23633624786), the article directly can be accessed simply by typing the IOI address in the internet browser. As example, find associated article to this IOI IOI.zone/23633624786 use following portal:            </p>                                                                                                 <h4>IOI Syntax Structure:</h4>                                            <p>An IOI consisted of two parts, zone name separated by an slash from 11 digit article identification number: IOI.zone/11 digits.                        IOI is 22 length including zone name as following example: IOI.zone/23633624786: </p>                                                                       </div>                                                <p>Publishers should explicitly display IOI in the described format on online published article. IOI cannot be any arbitrary number. It is a self-verifiable number by IOI-V3 algorithm. To check the validity of a IOI, IOI validator portal is used.                                                    <h4>History of Development of IOI:</h4>                                            <p>Uniform Resource Locator (URL) invented in 1989. Its drawback is that when the URL of an article is broken, the reader cannot access the article. To solve this problem, Direct Object Identifier (DOI) was introduced by DOI Foundation in the US in 1998.                        A IOI is an assigned fixed URL to an article\'s actual URL. Thus, if the actual article URL changes, the publisher should adjust the new URL of the article to its IOI URL via publisher account, and if the publisher does not adjust the new URL to the Article IOI URL, the IOI URL will not work.                        International Object Identifier (IOI) was introduced on 2014 by IOI-Agency Ltd in Germany as a permanent URL to article partly similar to DOI but with this difference that IOI address also keeps article metadata on a separate server from article server.                        If the actual article URL is broken IOI server detects it and will load the only article metadata instead with suggestions about possible new URLs of the article.                        Also, the publisher does not need to adjust the new URL of article manually,                       IOI server finds the new URL', '', '', 'auto', '', '', 'auto', '', '', 'auto', '', '', 'auto', '', '', 'auto', '', '', 'auto', '', '', 'auto', '', '', 'auto', '', 'ioi'),
