('international-library.org', 12, 'International Library', 0, 0, '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', '<br>The homepage of international-library.org was slow with loading time of 1.6ms at Apr 25 2023 12:22 for user with IP: 95.90.207.207', 'article_directory'),
('science-score.org', 13, 'Science Score', 0, 0, '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '<h4>International Author Grade</h4>                                                                   \r\n<h6>International Author Grade denoted by GRADE is a score computed for each author worldwide. \r\nGRADE is a score computed based on a check list. GRADE is an international score</h6>\r\n<h4>GRADE computed based following main criterion:                         </h4>                                              <div style=\"margin-top:15px; font-size:14px; line-height:20px\">  \r\n<ul><li>The number of author publication</li>\r\n<li>The scientific value of the publishers of the author article according to GRADE 72 item-check list</li>\r\n<li>The scientific value of the articles according to GRADE 32 item check list</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</div>   \r\n<h4>Accessing Author Publication by GRADE:</h4>\r\n<p>An GRADE not only is a score but also is a permanent address to an author publications list  (example: GRADE.ZONE/BC32-625), the author directly can be accessed simply by typing the GRADE address in the internet browser. As example, find articles published by an author with GRADE.ZONE/A28-28 use following portal:</p>                                                                                                 <h4>GRADE Syntax Structure:</h4>    \r\n<p>An GRADE consisted of two parts, zone name separated by an slash from a letter and 3 digit author identification number e.g. A828: in GRADE.ZONE/AK28-28. 28 is the score of the author out of 100. The maximum score is 100.\r\nGRADE is is 18 length including zone name as following example: GRADE.ZONE/A828-28: </p>                                                                       </div> \r\n<p>Authors should explicitly display GRADE in the described format in their CV, university profile, or social networks such as Linkedin.\r\n<h4>History of Development of GRADE:</h4>\r\n<p>Grading authors invented from the time impact factor introduced on 1956. Its drawback is that it cannot display all scientific value and skills of an author. Also, the authors can improve their articles and so their score. Thus, they have no control in their author score. GRADE was introduced on 2014 by GRADE-Agency in France as a permanent URL which is a score at the same time (score included at the end of the GRADE URL). This GRADE which is a score and URL allow author and anybody else to access the author publications. Not only author can see the list of his article using GRADE (simply by vising GRADE URL in browser), but also can modify his articles and improve their quality and score so increasing the author score. More importantly, other author and visitors worldwide can leave comment on each part of the author articles helps the author to improve his article based. </p>', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', NULL, '', '', '', 'author');

